f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}e & e & \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0 & e & e \\\\ 0 & 0 & e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414004 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414004 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414004 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414004 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $X=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${X}^{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a76ab5b-a059-5458-b5f4-e674816ed366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414004 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and the given matrix $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}^{5}=(PD{P}^{-1})^{5}=P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{D}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{5} & =P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -96 & 64\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175